WEBVTT

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:44.640
Big skies, big beaches, the biggest vistas.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:52.520
The coastline of Northumberland stretches for 70 glorious miles.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:58.320
It's wild and remote, and it's often empty, and it's somewhere that I keep coming back to.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:04.640
This part of the world might feel timeless, but the coastline is constantly changing.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:09.640
The power of the sea is ripping out great chunks, revealing ancient treasures.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.920
And there are treasures of a different kind waiting for me beneath the waves.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:16.440
One-two-three four-five-six-seven eight-nine...wow!

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.240
Tom's finding out about some pioneering pigs.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.800
You've heard of genetically modified crops - what about genetically modified animals?

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.600
These pigs are on a farm with the only GM livestock in Britain.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:37.200
It's all about tackling animal diseases, but would you eat the meat?

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.800
I'll be investigating.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.560
And Adam's looking forward to reaping what he's sown.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:49.560
With the crops on the farm, summer is a busy time of year with harvest just around the corner.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.800
But the crops and animals are very much dominated by the weather patterns we have throughout the year.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.680
So, I'm taking a look back to remind myself how difficult it's been over the last eight months.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.600
Northumberland is England's most northerly county.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:23.200
As a young man, my first reporting job for the BBC was in the nearest big city, Newcastle upon Tyne.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.840
At weekends, I'd head north to these beaches.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:36.880
I loved the sense of space and scale, and the dramatic remains of castles, reminders of this coastline's bloodthirsty history.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:51.440
Today, this land is once again under threat, not from Viking hordes or wild gangs of Border Reivers, but from the sea.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:58.280
Coastal erosion is hitting the east of the country hard.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.280
Storms are getting rougher.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.800
The land is taking a battering.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:09.480
Here, in this part of Northumberland, they're losing more than a metre of their coastline every year.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:22.280
But here at Druridge Bay coasted erosion is forcing the land to give up its secrets in a spectacular way.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.800
And those secrets are quite surprising.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:33.560
Archaeologist Clive Waddington and his mostly volunteer team are busy uncovering the past.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.120
Well, just what is this place revealing?

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:39.600
We've got this well-preserved Bronze Age cairn here.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.320
This is an old tomb, something like about 4,500 years ago.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:55.640
It's quite large, about 16 metres in diameter, but, unfortunately, we've lost about half of it to the sea, so the cliff edge here's been eaten away by the winter storms particularly.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.880
How was it discovered?

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:04.480
It was discovered by a man walking his dog here, and he saw the remains of one of these stone graves falling out of the cliff face, and realised there was human bones in it.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:13.560
So, he called the police and the police came with an archaeologist, and it was at that point we realised there was this large burial mound that was basically falling out of the cliff.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.920
So, you found lots of Bronze Age human remains - anything with them?

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.560
We found quite a bit of prehistoric pottery as well, so...

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.080
You can see this material.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:30.440
This one's rather nice, this is a piece of what we call food vessel, for the afterlife, for the journey into...

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.960
Yeah, you can see this is fingernail impressions.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.720
And it's in a kind of herringbone pattern.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.040
Very elaborate, really.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:41.840
Yeah, and that's about 4,000 years old.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:46.720
And this piece, this is either another kind of food vessel or a burial urn.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:51.240
And, typically, these are found upside down, sometimes with a cremation inside.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:57.440
The erosion is giving up more than just long-dead remains.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:00.320
Beneath the burial mound there's a story in the rocks themselves.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:06.360
What we've got here is this incredible sequence below the Bronze Age archaeology.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.480
It's like a layer cake.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:10.200
You can see the different layers of strata in there.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.160
This is very different, this is all little bits of stuff.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:22.560
It is, you can see it's very beach-rounded, we've got kind of gravel in there, but also larger blocks as well, and this would have resulted from a very powerful event.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:31.000
Basically it's a storm surge, so, today we'd call it a tsunami, and it's brought these large blocks up and thrown them high onto the shoreline...

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:36.080
Could that have been what created the North Sea and the English Channel?

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:47.520
Yes, we think this could date so far back, probably around 6,500BC, and that's the time when, we think, Britain finally got separated from the Continent, and this could have been the event that finally broke that land bridge.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.600
Today, there's an early sea fret and the waves are benign.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:06:05.320
Often they are tempestuous, but no matter what the conditions, each tide can reveal new secrets, a rich seam for the archaeologists and for, perhaps, an unexpected partner, the Northumberland Wildlife Trust.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:06.800
I'm joining Steve Low from the trust.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:12.480
So, how come you are involved in an archaeological project?

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:32.120
Well, John, we know a lot about the area at the moment - its environment, its wildlife - but we don't know much about how it's changed over time, and this dig is giving us a real window into the past to tell us more about what was here before, the animals and the people that lived here, and how they exploited them.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:41.160
At the moment, what I'm doing is removing this layer of sand from on top of the ancient peat bed to expose these footprints.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:42.880
What are you coming across?

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.360
That could be an ancient cattle footprint.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:44.800
Uh-huh.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:45.760
And this looks like a human footprint.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:50.800
You can see the heel there, the instep and the toes.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.920
Smaller footprint than mine!

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:56.360
What kind of person would that footprint have belonged to?

00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:10.040
We think that would have belonged to someone perhaps who lived in the dig nearby, a hunter-gatherer using the environment to gather all of their food, eating the wild boar, the deer and everything else we're finding in here.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:16.600
And it's really quite inspirational to think that that's been there for thousands of years, and here we are seeing it today.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:22.400
So, what is all this telling us about the way that Druridge Bay has changed over the many thousands of years?

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:30.160
This used to be part of the mainland, and now that is much further back from us.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:40.600
Coastal erosion is continuing to happen, and we try to fight it all of the time but, of course, I think we need to recognise we can't always be successful with that.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:48.760
Erosion here is opening up for us an extraordinary window into our past.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:57.880
But sites like these are vulnerable, for many of the secrets they keep are being lost to the sea all up and down our east coast.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:06.200
Here at Druridge Bay, at least there's a chance to learn something before all trace is lost.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:13.480
Now, the idea of growing GM crops in the UK is hugely controversial.

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:16.560
But what about the genetic modification of animals?

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:17.200
Tom has been investigating.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:24.760
The lowlands of Scotland.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:28.720
A wealth of natural beauty.

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:36.679
Untouched, you might think, by interference from the modern world.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:38.720
But appearances can be deceptive.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:44.760
Just over there, nestling amongst the trees is Roslin, home to some extraordinary scientific breakthroughs.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:55.040
Dolly the cloned sheep was created there, and now another radical achievement - full of potential, but stalked by controversy.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:05.160
We've been arguing about the rights and wrongs of GM crops for 30 years.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:12.840
And in Europe, they're so tightly regulated, one of the leading biotech companies, Monsanto, has just scrapped plans to develop new GM varieties in the EU.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:22.160
But here, at the Roslin Institute, south of Edinburgh, little noticed by the opponents of GM, some remarkable things have been happening.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:26.800
This is the tail of a pig, but no ordinary pig.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:32.480
She's the first farm animal in the UK produced using new gene-editing technology.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:36.240
She goes by the grand name of Pig 26.

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:39.640
So, which one is home to Pig 26?

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:45.080
'Chief swineherd - 'well, professor of biotechnology - is Bruce Whitelaw.'

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:45.760
What do you think, ready for some feed?

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:48.920
I would imagine they are.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:57.920
Pig 26 has been genetically engineered to have a very precise mutation in one specific gene.

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:03.120
The key thing of that mutation is exactly where we want it to be in one of the 20,000 genes this animal has.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:06.240
What problem are you seeking to solve?

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:09.200
Pigs suffer from many diseases.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:14.080
One in particular is a swine fever, and the disease we are looking at is African swine fever.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:18.040
The African pig can tolerate this virus - it does not die.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:23.760
If one of these animals was infected by African swine fever virus, it would bleed to death within a couple of days.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:29.640
No vaccine, no drugs to treat it.

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:31.000
African swine fever is spreading.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:37.480
It's already rampant in Russia and, it's feared, could reach here.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:41.240
How far down the line are you to making Pig 26 resistant?

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:43.960
If the disease came here, would she be immune?

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:44.640
She would probably not be immune.

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:46.320
There are two steps to this project.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:57.440
One is showing we can actually target a specific sequence in that specific gene, and that's what Pig 26 demonstrates.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:06.600
The next challenge is to change one base in that to the base normally found in the African pig, and we're working on that just now.

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:14.040
The traditional genetically modifying or engineering technology involves transferring a whole gene or even a hybrid gene, sometimes between species.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:20.000
This technology allows us to manipulate the endogenous gene very precisely and very subtly.

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:22.200
Some genes contain thousands of bases.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:26.320
We just want to change one single base.

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:35.040
It's taken the scientists at Roslin years to get this far, but when you realise the complexity of what they are trying to do, you start to see why.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:38.640
To make sense of it, I'm going back to school - in fact, pre-school.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:48.760
Imagine this represents the inside of a fertilised egg taken from a pregnant sow.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:49.200
Now, a bit of a stretch, I know.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:51.920
In reality, it would be smaller than a pinhead.

00:11:51.120 --> 00:12:05.880
And each one of those fertilised eggs would contain 20,000 genes, like the balls here, and each one of those would control a characteristic like the length of the tail or maybe the risk of catching a certain disease.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:11.320
But finding the gene you want to modify is just the first step.

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:32.120
Each gene contains a string of DNA, and only by cutting that in exactly the right place do you achieve the affect that you want, and that's exactly what the scientists did at Roslin, admittedly, using chemistry, not scissors... ..before putting the new strand of DNA back in with the other genes.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:42.400
When that fertilised egg grows into an embryo and eventually a pig, every cell in its body contains that altered gene.

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:56.760
With a process as complicated as this, it's hardly surprising that creating a pig that's resistant to swine fever is yet to become a reality, but that doesn't mean it can't be done.

00:12:55.960 --> 00:12:58.960
And these birds are the proof.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:03.880
Here at Roslin, they've not only genetically modified pigs.

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:09.680
They've created GM chickens, and these guys have some very special qualities.

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:23.160
They're part of a GM experiment to eradicate bird flu, which can be passed to humans, so the work of Dr Lucy Freem and her colleagues is of, potentially, global importance.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:39.320
Excitingly, we have already developed a chicken that is partially resistant to bird flu, so these chickens, if they catch bird flu, do get sick and die, but they don't pass the virus on to other chickens kept in the same pen or, potentially, on to humans, so that's already a really big advance.

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:45.440
So, we are trying to improve on that and make a chicken that's fully resistant to bird flu, that when exposed to it is resistant to catching it as well.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:56.640
The team at Roslin is even starting work on the highly complex task of tackling foot-and-mouth disease.

00:13:55.840 --> 00:14:02.480
So, could gene editing be a major part of animal health in years to come?

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:07.240
It has the potential, but it's only one of the potential solutions.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:16.800
We have huge research going into vaccine technology, drug development, husbandry, management of the animals in general.

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:28.560
All that will come together and give us our solutions to these diseases in the future, but genetic engineering has an opportunity to contribute to that.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:36.440
Genetically modified livestock is confined to the experimental farm.

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:45.320
Unlike GM crops, there aren't commercial varieties ready to go, so broad public opinion is largely unformed.

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:47.720
But some objections are starting to emerge, as I'll be finding out later.

00:14:54.400 --> 00:15:12.800
Back in May, Ellie was in Portsmouth, a town long associated with the Royal Navy, but out beyond the warships are massive intertidal mudflats, home to some of our rarest creatures.

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:16.240
But crossing those mudflats to see them?

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:19.960
This is Fareham Creek.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:24.120
At low tide, the mudflats stretch as far as the eye can see.

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:32.080
I'm on the hunt for one of our most threatened native shellfish.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:35.120
But, to find out if they're here, I need to head out there.

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:37.200
Not as easy as you might think.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:43.040
What I'm hoping to see are native oysters.

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:46.680
In its heyday, the Solent had one of the largest natural populations.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:54.120
They were harvested and shipped to London and Paris to meet the demands of high society.

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:59.480
But since 2000, they've been in decline.

00:15:58.720 --> 00:16:06.880
Jolyon Chesworth is running a project to try and gauge accurate numbers.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:08.640
Some extraordinary footwear here.

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:09.200
That's right.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:13.720
These are mud shoes and it's what we wear when we go out onto the mudflats and carry out surveys, to help stop us sinking.

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:25.280
They're based on the design of herons' feet, and they have these expandable wings so when you put your foot down, the pressure lifts the wings up and helps evenly spread your weight and stops you going too far into the mud.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:29.960
That's the wrong way round, Ellie, you need to turn them round and slip them on like normal shoes.

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:30.600
Blimey.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:30.720
There's nothing normal about these shoes.

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:35.760
There we go.

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:36.000
Perfect.

00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:38.480
Shall we give them a try?

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:41.680
S I feel like a clown.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:49.040
It's tough going in the mud, but it's the only way to find them.

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:59.560
Strewth, this is loads of work.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:02.200
Why do you do it?

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:22.119
This area's been recommended for protection through a marine conservation zone for native oysters, but it hasn't progressed cos there's a lack of evidence to suggest they are here in enough numbers to actually designate this site, so what we're going to be doing, over the coming months, is to be going out and looking for them, monitoring them and actually gathering the evidence to make sure this site does get designated.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:25.319
We're not going to do the whole patch today?

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:25.920
No, we're just going to have a little recce.

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:26.319
Cool.

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:28.160
We're against the tide as well, so let's get on with it, shall we?

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:39.920
For a full survey, Jolyon will be out here for up to six hours and will cover around two miles.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:44.920
What you have found there is a Pacific oyster and a large one at that.

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:55.240
The reason you can tell the difference between a Pacific and a native is the Pacific oysters have quite a sharp point and they fan out and they have a very corrugated rim.

00:17:54.360 --> 00:18:00.520
The native oysters, as we will hopefully find later, are a lot smoother and flatter.

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:08.240
Jolyon records all Pacific oyster sightings to keep track of their numbers too, because they're a known invasive species.

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:08.960
Are they a problem for our native oysters?

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:14.360
In some areas, possibly, in this area we don't have them in such numbers that they're likely to compete.

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:22.680
Also, the native oysters prefer deeper water whereas these prefer shallow, intertidal areas, so their ranges don't necessarily overlap.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:27.000
It doesn't take long before we find what we're looking for.

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:29.200
Here we've got a native oyster.

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:32.960
Sometimes called a flat oyster because it has a very flat shell to it.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:34.680
You can see it's quite a different shape...

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:35.480
Yes.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:39.400
It's much rounder, much smoother.

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:44.280
Obviously, this one's a lot smaller, but that's because it's quite a few years younger than this one.

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:46.560
These can get, like you say, quite a bit bigger, though, so the size isn't the giveaway.

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:52.880
This is probably a year or two old, whereas that one is more like seven years.

00:18:52.880 --> 00:19:03.640
Hopefully, Jolyon can find enough native oysters to get these mudflats protected, ensuring their survival for years to come.

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:14.840
On the other side of Portsmouth harbour, a once-thriving oyster farm lies dormant.

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:20.400
Back in the 1900s, this would have been a hive of fishing activity.

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:27.000
But pollution brought business to a standstill after the First World War.

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:40.400
Today, this man-made farm now plays host to thousands of birds rather than oysters, including one of our rarest sea birds, the little tern.

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:43.520
The RSPB are using some rather unusual methods to try and protect it.

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:46.240
And that's where this beast comes in.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:20:01.480
These are cockleshells and it's on this surface that little terns like to nest, but because they're fairly particular, they prefer to nest on this - crushed-up cockleshells.

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:06.720
Hence the roller.

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:13.480
All bagged up, I head to the oyster beds with RSPB warden Wes Smith.

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:16.320
What's happening with this lot, then?

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:27.640
We're going to get this lot across to the island here where we're going to put the shells out and create some perfect nesting material for the little terns.

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:32.720
Little terns only visit the UK in the summer, to nest and rear their young.

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.400
This is the perfect time to do it, right now.

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:39.400
We had two circling overhead today.

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:40.520
So, they're just on their way back.

00:20:39.640 --> 00:20:42.800
The little tern has been in decline right across Europe.

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:50.000
During the summer months, 8% of the UK's entire population are found right here.

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:54.400
Why this island as opposed to anywhere else along this coast?

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:56.040
Well, this one here is currently empty.

00:20:55.160 --> 00:21:02.760
We've got some black-headed gulls and Mediterranean gulls on some of the islands which are very suitable for them.

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:12.960
Little terns, being small birds, they tend to get bullied, pushed out of the main cluster, so this one here, if we can get it just at this timing, it will be absolutely perfect for them.

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:18.200
Finally, it's time to help volunteer warden Chris Coburn cover the newly weeded surface with shells.

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:23.880
Then, some hard landscaping is needed.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:27.960
What's with the bricks, Chris?

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:29.720
Well, this looks very uniform.

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:35.920
So, you're a little tern, you're coming back to your nest - where is it?

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:36.120
I would never know.

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:37.720
Right, so let's have a little marker.

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:39.520
Are we playing boules with the bricks?

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:43.600
Yeah.

00:21:43.000 --> 00:21:44.400
Just anywhere?

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:46.400
A bit of set dressing to try and really encourage them in?

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:51.200
If you've got little terns nesting here, they've now got identifiable locations - they go flying over, "Ah, that's my brick.

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:54.760
"Now, where was my nest?"

00:21:53.920 --> 00:21:55.480
And this is where we're very cunning.

00:21:54.520 --> 00:21:55.800
Oh!

00:21:54.920 --> 00:21:59.560
I'm going to put down some decoys to see if we can attract them.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:01.640
So, these handmade jobs are life-size...

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:03.520
Shows how small they are.

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:03.680
That's why they're called little terns.

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:05.280
Yeah, yeah.

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:09.120
Does this work, putting a decoy in?

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:15.600
In America, they've moved colonies of 2,000 birds.

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:16.000
It's taken a little while, but...

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:16.280
By using these decoys?

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:17.000
By using these decoys it just works a treat.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:21.040
With the decoys set, the only thing left to do is wait.

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:30.880
Hopefully, these lifelike models will transform these derelict oyster beds into a little-tern haven.

00:22:38.200 --> 00:22:44.560
There's more to Northumberland than its long swathes of sandy beaches and wide-open skies.

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:50.080
There's all the drama of the National Park, its brooding valleys and sweeping hillsides.

00:22:49.840 --> 00:22:54.000
And down there, hidden amongst the trees, is something unique in the British landscape.

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:04.760
A graveyard of sorts, of once proud metal.

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:09.720
Row upon row of rusting machines that used to dominate harvest time.

00:23:08.920 --> 00:23:14.920
So many that you can see them from space.

00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:27.360
They all belong to farmer John Manners.

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:30.160
350 combine harvesters.

00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:32.480
John's taking farming diversification to a new level.

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:37.560
John, this is an amazing sight.

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:40.920
I've never seen anything like it, 350 dead combine harvesters.

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:44.760
Yes, well, they're not going to go again.

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:46.800
When they get here, I'm called the undertaker.

00:23:45.960 --> 00:23:51.400
JOHN CRAVEN CHUCKLES How did it all start?

00:23:50.560 --> 00:24:11.600
I needed some wheels and tyres for a tractor which were very expensive, and I found this combine which was local... ..and I got it for the same price as the wheels and tyres so I brought it home, took the wheels and tyres off, put them onto my tractor and then somebody came wanting the engine, somebody came wanting something else, and it just mushroomed.

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:11.880
It grewed and grewed!

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:13.520
Yes, it did.

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:16.200
It just went on and on and on.

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:29.920
That one looks as though it's been on fire.

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:32.480
Yes, that's a fire-damaged combine.

00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:33.040
Is it a common thing for combines to burn out?

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:35.040
Oh, yes, it is.

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:37.720
You've got to look after them.

00:24:36.760 --> 00:24:46.560
Hose them out, blow them out, clean the engines out cos of the dust and the muck and everything.

00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:58.480
Cos a brand-new combine is incredibly expensive, isn't it?

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:03.040
About 300,000 for the top of the range.

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:08.840
So, it's understandable why people want a lot of spares.

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:11.920
Well, I don't cater for the new boy.

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:15.480
I cater for the smaller market...the banger market, should we say, in the combine world.

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:28.040
And how much - can you tell me - how much do you pay for a combine?

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:28.880
I can't tell you that!

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:31.040
BOTH LAUGH Trade secret.

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:32.560
It's business, isn't it?

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:37.080
Competitors might start!

00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:55.360
Earlier, we heard how, deep in the Scottish countryside, scientists have produced Britain's first genetically modified farm animals.

00:25:54.520 --> 00:26:00.160
But, as Tom's been finding out, not everyone's comfortable with the idea.

00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:05.200
Genetically modified pigs that can't catch swine fever?

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:09.840
GM chickens that can't catch bird flu?

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:16.880
This is the kind of future that scientists at the Roslin Institute are working towards.

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:23.920
But if GM animals become part of the future of British farming, it raises some questions.

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:27.080
How would farmers feel about rearing them?

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:30.200
How would the Government regulate them?

00:26:29.320 --> 00:26:42.280
And, perhaps most importantly, how would you and I, the consumers, feel about eating lamb from a GM sheep or bacon from a genetically engineered pig?

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:51.320
At Stagehall Farm in the Scottish Borders, they rear cattle the conventional way.

00:26:50.480 --> 00:26:52.080
Most of the calves are Angus.

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:57.560
What we are looking for is shorthorn cows and a lot of Angus to sell.

00:26:56.880 --> 00:26:59.200
So, most of the calves are black, as you see.

00:26:59.280 --> 00:27:00.800
Hello.

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:01.440
Has he got a name?

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:03.680
He's Elwood.

00:27:02.720 --> 00:27:04.600
He was bred here, he's got a brother as well.

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:06.440
Him and his brother are very similar.

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:22.920
Elwood's family is the result of generations of careful cross-breeding by beef farmer Nigel Miller, president of the National Farmers' Union in Scotland.

00:27:22.120 --> 00:27:23.600
Come on!

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:23.760
Come on, there's a bit of food here, too.

00:27:22.840 --> 00:27:30.600
He thinks traditional methods have worked well, but feels there is room for the precision of GM.

00:27:29.800 --> 00:27:38.400
With this sort of very precise technology, you can start looking at health and welfare issues and building them into our breeds.

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:42.360
One of the things they said they were just beginning to work on at Roslin was foot-and-mouth disease.

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:44.440
What would you think if they did something with that?

00:27:44.200 --> 00:27:46.960
Well, that would be a miraculous change for me.

00:27:46.120 --> 00:27:53.120
If you could break out of the whole problem of foot-and-mouth, it'd be fantastic.

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:56.240
Nigel does have his reservations, though.

00:27:55.520 --> 00:27:59.600
He's worried about the possible influence of big business.

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:03.360
We don't want this to be a commercial lock on breeding of livestock.

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:08.280
So, you're worried, are you, if someone gets their patent on the super cow?

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:09.960
And that they could then control everything?

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:17.160
Yeah, I think that's a real fear, and that's one of the reasons why genetic modification in plants has got a bad name, and I don't think we want to go there.

00:28:16.320 --> 00:28:24.160
We don't want to have farmers or communities held to ransom by a commercial organisation.

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:29.960
But for others, the objections go much deeper.

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:42.080
Environmental scientist Dr Helen Wallace is from GeneWatch - an organisation with concerns about GM crops and animals.

00:28:41.240 --> 00:28:48.880
Here at Roslin, not far behind us, they are actually working on animals that are resistant to disease.

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:49.400
Surely that's good for the animals' welfare?

00:28:48.600 --> 00:29:06.760
Well, there's not very much understood about how that resistance might work in practice, so one concern is that disease-resistant animals could act as a reservoir for the virus, be infected and pass it on to other animals but without you knowing.

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:10.760
And when it comes to eating this meat, which, of course, we aren't doing yet - what are your worries there?

00:29:10.040 --> 00:29:15.560
I think it will be up to consumers to decide whether they really want to eat this meat.

00:29:14.680 --> 00:29:28.040
They'll have concerns about food safety because it's difficult to provide definitive evidence that the changes in the meat, or the changes in the milk, are safe for humans in the longer term.

00:29:27.400 --> 00:29:34.000
These small fry are the first GM animals anyone's likely to eat.

00:29:33.160 --> 00:29:41.160
American scientists are genetically modifying salmon so they grow bigger faster.

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:44.440
And they could go on sale over there later this year.

00:29:43.680 --> 00:29:51.120
So, what will that mean here in Scotland where salmon has spawned a huge industry?

00:29:50.560 --> 00:30:00.320
Salmon and Trout Association chairman Hugh Campbell Adamson says GM fish pose many threats to wild salmon.

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:02.080
The worst one is getting genetics muddled up with our wild fish.

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:20.720
And these wonderful fish we have that swim thousands of miles yet still get back to their original river and still do that extraordinary life cycle - there's a real danger, I think, and a lot of people would think of these fish being polluted to a degree by genes coming in artificially.

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:24.960
But if they are properly contained, as they say, in tanks away from the sea, surely that's not a problem?

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:35.520
If you could swear to me, Tom, that every single fish is going to stay inside that cage and never get into the wild, and that was to be proved, I think a lot of the problems would go.

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:39.600
But I don't think our record, as humans, on this is particularly good.

00:30:38.680 --> 00:30:52.280
Back at Roslin, Britain's only genetically modified livestock are safely behind bars, including these latest additions, GM sheep.

00:30:52.200 --> 00:30:56.440
So, what's the view here of contaminating the gene pool?

00:30:55.640 --> 00:31:02.480
If, for example, Pig 26 escaped and bred with ordinary farm pigs?

00:31:01.600 --> 00:31:06.760
All it would do is transfer disease resistance to that animal.

00:31:05.920 --> 00:31:12.720
But, as far as people who don't like GM are concerned, you have then got a GM-contaminated stock of pigs.

00:31:11.840 --> 00:31:21.360
So, it's a natural mutation we have with Pig 26, it's a normal mutation which could be found in that population.

00:31:20.520 --> 00:31:21.720
We've engineered it into Pig 26.

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:27.960
It is possible that some time in the future a natural mating would produce that mutation.

00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:35.560
There's no additional risk to a GM as in Pig 26 compared to that natural mutation.

00:31:34.760 --> 00:31:45.080
There's no doubt it's a challenging thought, the idea of GM livestock grazing these fields, sentient beings whose genes we've tweaked.

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:53.280
But cutting disease and boosting productivity is a huge prize, leaving us plenty to chew on.

00:31:55.440 --> 00:32:01.800
You never know what any season will bring until Mother Nature plays her hand.

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:11.200
The hot summer finally arrived last month and for Adam it was time to make hay while the sun shone.

00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:32.680
Come on, lambs.

00:32:31.760 --> 00:32:33.800
Come on, lambs.

00:32:34.840 --> 00:32:44.760
The pastures have been flourishing in the much-needed sunshine, which is great for the livestock and for my sanity.

00:32:43.960 --> 00:32:48.280
I thought the British summer was a thing of the past.

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:54.000
And this field has got a lot of lovely natural shade and water.

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:54.680
And, as the temperature's warming up, these lambs need it.

00:32:55.760 --> 00:33:02.320
In the summer months, the pasture in this valley is valuable grazing.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:06.920
The moist soil combined with lots of sunshine is ideal for grass growth.

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:10.320
But earlier in the year it was a different story.

00:33:09.480 --> 00:33:16.440
I hoped that when we turned the corner from winter to spring we'd have perfect growing conditions.

00:33:15.680 --> 00:33:17.720
But it wasn't to be.

00:33:16.760 --> 00:33:18.200
Spring was dreadful.

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:24.240
When we were expecting the weather to warm up, it stayed cold and rained a lot.

00:33:23.360 --> 00:33:26.360
There was no sunshine.

00:33:25.440 --> 00:33:34.240
And it had an immense effect across the UK and particularly in Ireland where they had a lack of fodder or feed for their sheep and cattle.

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:39.040
And because the grass didn't grow, the ewes weren't producing milk and the lambs suffered.

00:33:38.240 --> 00:33:44.920
We weren't too badly affected here but in some parts of the country, sheep were dying and dying in their hundreds.

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:51.400
I visited Welsh sheep farmer Errol Morris.

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:55.840
He lost nearly 200 sheep during the prolonged heavy snow in April.

00:33:55.720 --> 00:33:58.520
Is that a dead one up there?

00:33:57.680 --> 00:34:00.040
Ah, there's another one there, yes.

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:07.480
When the storm came they ran for shelter and the walls were where they went.

00:34:06.520 --> 00:34:09.760
And have they died mainly of the cold?

00:34:09.040 --> 00:34:12.800
Yes, it's the cold and suffocation in the drifts.

00:34:11.920 --> 00:34:22.080
If they are under the snow at this...like concrete...they have no hope, have they?

00:34:23.120 --> 00:34:28.360
Eventually, the snow cleared, spring arrived and it was onwards and upwards.

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:30.040
It's amazing how the tides can turn.

00:34:29.239 --> 00:34:39.600
July brought sunshine and plenty of it and I didn't meet a single farmer who wasn't pleased to see the warmer weather and for the grassland it was perfect to get it growing.

00:34:40.760 --> 00:34:45.040
For the sheep it couldn't have come a moment sooner.

00:34:44.120 --> 00:34:52.719
At last, the lush green pasture bloomed, the grass flourished and the fresh food supply for my animals was plentiful.

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:56.760
And it wasn't just the grazing pastures like this one that improved.

00:34:56.040 --> 00:35:02.480
Some of the other fields were in perfect condition too so it was all hands to the deck to make hay while the sun shone.

00:35:06.200 --> 00:35:11.720
There's usually only a small window when hay is at the right stage for harvesting.

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:13.680
And that came in July.

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:22.360
The grass was cut and left to dry out in the fields.

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:27.000
This hay turner helps accelerate that process by turning the grass to remove moisture.

00:35:27.480 --> 00:35:32.480
In this fine weather it's turned twice a day.

00:35:31.680 --> 00:35:37.720
Hay can be made in around four days and that's exactly what we did.

00:35:37.800 --> 00:35:42.480
And as soon as it's ready it's baled for storage.

00:35:42.880 --> 00:35:50.760
This machine compresses the hay into bales making it easy to handle, transport and store.

00:35:50.000 --> 00:35:55.280
Once it's done eight it releases them together.

00:35:54.480 --> 00:35:58.960
Then we bring in a tractor with a front loader to collect them.

00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:03.040
And that's when the real hard graft starts.

00:36:02.480 --> 00:36:08.040
The farm team have to lug and stack the bales in hot and dusty conditions.

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:10.680
It's no easy task.

00:36:09.920 --> 00:36:12.440
But it's a job well done.

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:27.680
We had more grass than we expected this year so the hay stores are brimful and we've got some surplus hay to sell which is absolutely brilliant, it's a bit of a bonus.

00:36:26.800 --> 00:36:28.640
This hay's going off to Wales.

00:36:31.320 --> 00:36:36.080
It's quite an art, loading these lorries, so they're safe.

00:36:35.120 --> 00:36:37.600
Yes, got to be safe to go down the road.

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:38.400
And how many will you get on?

00:36:37.600 --> 00:36:40.960
It will be 50.

00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:44.320
But, there's a lot of grass about this year so the price has been falling, hasn't it?

00:36:43.360 --> 00:36:49.360
Yeah, the weather's been so good everybody has made hay rather than silage.

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:49.880
Yeah.

00:36:50.080 --> 00:36:51.320
So it means there's a lot of hay about.

00:36:50.360 --> 00:36:54.200
You're buying this at £60 a tonne?

00:36:53.360 --> 00:36:55.440
And what will you sell it on for?

00:36:54.560 --> 00:36:57.800
Hopefully 80 or something around there, as much as I can get.

00:36:57.000 --> 00:36:59.560
And that will pay for your haulage and make a living as well?

00:36:58.600 --> 00:36:59.840
Hopefully, yes.

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:05.200
It's really important that the load is totally secure for the motorway.

00:37:04.480 --> 00:37:11.880
So, the driver's just put a strap across and he'll hold that bale on so that Dave can back out and get his spikes out of the bale.

00:37:14.720 --> 00:37:18.080
Well done, Dave.

00:37:17.120 --> 00:37:18.400
Good skills.

00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:29.280
Hay needs to be stored indoors but as we don't have enough barn space, we make silage too that can be kept outside.

00:37:28.360 --> 00:37:32.280
This is a stack of silage bales.

00:37:31.480 --> 00:37:46.200
Silage is similar to hay in that they are both grass that are mown and then made into the product but hay is kept very dry.

00:37:45.440 --> 00:38:04.360
And silage is baled when the grass is still a bit green and a bit younger so it's higher in protein and sugars and it was only three weeks ago that the contractors were out in the field working hard and in those rows of grass, the baler goes along and creates the bale and that's followed by the wrapper, a really clever machine that picks up the bale and spins it and while it's being spun the wrap is going round and round the bale and it has two or three wraps of plastic all on it.

00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:16.080
And what's happening inside here - the plastic has created anaerobic conditions, so with no air.

00:38:15.360 --> 00:38:27.600
And the grass is starting to ferment and pickle and that means it can be stored for over a year and we feed it to the cattle and sheep in the winter.

00:38:26.760 --> 00:38:29.040
It's a really good feed.

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:37.080
One of the downsides is that we end up with lots of plastic but now we recycle it and we send it to a company that make it into chicken houses.

00:38:36.200 --> 00:38:37.360
In fact, my dog kennels were made out of it.

00:38:36.520 --> 00:38:42.840
HE WHISTLES Here, dogs.

00:38:43.800 --> 00:38:49.720
While this has been a success story, the same can't be said for our arable crops.

00:38:48.920 --> 00:39:00.960
The oilseed rape suffered during the prolonged cold spring and it wasn't until the end of June that it finally flowered, a month later than normal.

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:11.200
The crop has now started to set seed and in this pod - if you open it up you can see the little green seeds that will turn red and then black ready for harvest.

00:39:10.400 --> 00:39:17.920
And this spring oilseed rape replaced the failed winter crop so I'm relying on it to do quite well.

00:39:16.960 --> 00:39:27.520
It's grown reasonably well and we're probably a few weeks off harvest, but the ground has started to crack and what we need now to help these seeds grow a bit more is some more rain.

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:31.360
And it gets worse.

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:38.480
In another field of oilseed rape it's not just the weather that was the problem, it's the common poppy.

00:39:37.640 --> 00:39:46.840
About a month ago this oilseed rape was in flower and the flowers are bright yellow but they were mixed with red, cos it was also full of poppies.

00:39:45.960 --> 00:39:51.000
Lovely if you are a photographer, not so good if you're a farmer.

00:39:50.400 --> 00:40:05.680
And this oilseed rape struggled in the spring and usually the leaf makes a canopy and competes with all the weeds around it, but because it was quite thin, the weeds have come through so now it's got all sorts in it.

00:40:04.880 --> 00:40:09.840
There's thistles, these daisies, we've got rayless mayweed and then the poppies.

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:15.600
And that's going to cause a bit of a problem at harvest but, also, in next year's crop.

00:40:14.800 --> 00:40:26.600
So, if I grab a poppy seed...you can see there's the seed head... and in there are thousands of little seeds that will shed onto the soil and grow next year.

00:40:25.640 --> 00:40:33.040
So that's going to be a bit of a challenge when we are growing wheat in here that we are going to plant in September, October time.

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:45.640
My barley also had a tough start in the spring but it made a great recovery.

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:46.960
Well, that was until the heat wave came.

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:51.760
The intense heat and dry weather back in July really set this crop back.

00:40:50.840 --> 00:41:00.200
It's still doing OK, it's flowered and now it's got the seeds growing in the head and this will be ready in about three weeks' time.

00:40:59.440 --> 00:41:06.000
And this grain, once it's harvested, will go for making beer.

00:41:05.240 --> 00:41:11.400
And the straw that's left behind is quite a good animal feed.

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:24.480
Farmers are always moaning about the weather - it's either too hot or too cold, too wet or too dry - but when it comes to the weather we really are in the lap of the gods.

00:41:23.600 --> 00:41:26.880
And we have to deal with whatever Mother Nature throws at us.

00:41:28.080 --> 00:41:38.080
Next week, I'm shopping for a special animal that's from one of the oldest rare breeds in the world.

00:41:42.800 --> 00:41:49.720
Today, I'm in Northumberland and soon I'll be heading out into the North Sea.

00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:55.280
It's rich in natural resources and home to an astonishing range of wildlife.

00:41:54.400 --> 00:42:01.320
It's here you'll find England's largest colony of grey seals thriving in the waters around the Farne Islands.

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:09.240
And each year hundreds of thousands of sea birds arrive here to breed.

00:42:11.120 --> 00:42:21.840
But today I'm going in search of a creature out there whose life we know very little about.

00:42:22.960 --> 00:42:32.800
Underwater cameraman Ben Burville is at the start of a five-year project to learn more about the life of this elusive mammal.

00:42:32.920 --> 00:42:36.680
What are the chances of us seeing this creature?

00:42:35.840 --> 00:42:38.160
With nature you never know, John.

00:42:37.520 --> 00:42:39.480
Fingers crossed!

00:42:41.600 --> 00:42:50.200
Our high-speed rib will take us far out to sea to an area where they've been spotted in the past.

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:53.160
So, what exactly is it that we're looking for?

00:42:52.200 --> 00:42:55.720
What we are looking for today, John, is this.

00:42:55.680 --> 00:43:06.120
This is a white-beaked dolphin, the most abundant of dolphins in the North Sea with about 8,000 to 10,000 of them in there but one that very few people know much about.

00:43:05.880 --> 00:43:09.240
Why's that?

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:13.920
It's really the fact that it tends to be in deeper waters and tends to be offshore.

00:43:13.040 --> 00:43:16.400
Is it important now to find out more about these dolphins?

00:43:15.560 --> 00:43:25.360
It is important for their conservation and also to find out whether activities that we do can affect them in an adverse way.

00:43:28.760 --> 00:43:37.240
We're passing close to the Farne Islands so I want to make a short stop to catch up on an important project there.

00:43:37.560 --> 00:43:40.560
For just a little while our quest for white-peaked dolphins is put on hold.

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:44.040
Welcome to the Farne Islands.

00:43:43.240 --> 00:43:46.080
Thank you very much, nice to be back.

00:43:45.120 --> 00:43:46.320
Lovely weather at the moment, isn't it?

00:43:46.880 --> 00:43:54.920
I was here five years ago, right at the start of a crucial puffin count.

00:43:54.840 --> 00:44:00.000
Indeed, good luck.

00:43:59.160 --> 00:44:04.560
What the count showed was that puffin numbers had collapsed.

00:44:03.800 --> 00:44:06.800
Ornithologists were fearful for the future.

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:11.840
Five years on and the latest survey has just ended.

00:44:11.320 --> 00:44:13.080
Is the picture any better?

00:44:12.200 --> 00:44:16.760
In 2008, the population had declined by about a third from the last census.

00:44:15.920 --> 00:44:19.960
Also, this winter was a very, very tough winter.

00:44:19.080 --> 00:44:22.800
It was a cold winter, a lot of onshore winds, and we had a big puffin wreck.

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:30.680
A puffin wreck basically involves birds washing up on the coast.

00:44:29.720 --> 00:44:40.400
So people were finding birds all along the north-east coast from northern Scotland down to Yorkshire.

00:44:40.600 --> 00:44:43.840
So, what about this year's census?

00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:50.760
This year's census is good news - we've gone up around 8% on top of what we had to around about 40,000 pairs of puffins on these islands.

00:44:50.760 --> 00:44:57.360
The census may be over but work goes on to discover more about these colourful birds.

00:44:56.640 --> 00:45:02.360
You are a brave man, Graeme, I've done that once and I got a very nasty bite!

00:45:02.040 --> 00:45:03.120
They are pretty vicious, I hope it's just a chick.

00:45:02.160 --> 00:45:04.040
I've got a nibble.

00:45:03.080 --> 00:45:06.160
It feels like a chick, I'll just bring it out now.

00:45:05.280 --> 00:45:06.840
And you've got a few hundred of these to do.

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:07.600
Indeed.

00:45:06.960 --> 00:45:10.440
A few hundred bites and scratches.

00:45:09.680 --> 00:45:12.000
There he is.

00:45:11.480 --> 00:45:13.080
Little fellow here.

00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:16.040
How old will that chick be?

00:45:17.600 --> 00:45:26.320
He's probably around the 35-day mark, he's pretty much ready to fledge, he's got his head cleared of down, just a few tufts here.

00:45:25.480 --> 00:45:28.480
So now you are going to put a ring on him.

00:45:27.600 --> 00:45:28.840
We are.

00:45:27.960 --> 00:45:29.800
We'll get a ring and, hopefully, get some good data.

00:45:29.840 --> 00:45:32.640
And what information do you hope to get from the ringing?

00:45:31.800 --> 00:45:34.040
The basic thing is how long they are living for.

00:45:33.240 --> 00:45:42.480
So if this bird comes back in future years and we re-catch it, we know when it was ringed, we know its exact ring number so it's got its own identity.

00:45:42.840 --> 00:45:44.040
And we know exactly how old it is.

00:45:43.120 --> 00:45:45.080
Pop him back in again.

00:45:44.600 --> 00:45:51.800
Back in the hole and he'll be happy as Larry and he will be out in a few days and head out into the Atlantic Ocean.

00:45:50.920 --> 00:45:52.480
And when would he come back again?

00:45:51.760 --> 00:45:58.720
Maybe in around about four years' time usually to get mature and start breeding for the first time.

00:45:58.240 --> 00:46:02.520
But until then he'll just be floating around.

00:46:05.240 --> 00:46:14.280
So, it's nice to know there's good news for the puffins here, photogenic, approachable little birds whose numbers are on the up.

00:46:14.640 --> 00:46:22.760
Everyone loves puffins but there's one bird species here on the Farnes which is nowhere near as friendly.

00:46:21.960 --> 00:46:25.800
Just ask Ellie.

00:46:24.960 --> 00:46:32.480
She was here a few weeks ago filming for a brand-new BBC One wildlife series.

00:46:31.640 --> 00:46:40.560
And she had a rather nasty encounter with arctic terns, the bad-tempered cousins of the little terns she met earlier on this programme.

00:46:40.000 --> 00:46:43.120
Oh!

00:46:42.960 --> 00:46:45.160
Oh, my goodness.

00:46:44.280 --> 00:46:45.600
They are really unhappy.

00:46:44.640 --> 00:46:45.880
Wah!

00:46:44.920 --> 00:46:46.880
Ahh!

00:46:46.000 --> 00:46:48.480
Got me.

00:46:48.040 --> 00:46:50.040
Ow!

00:46:50.480 --> 00:46:53.560
This is not fun.

00:46:52.600 --> 00:46:54.320
Not fun.

00:46:53.440 --> 00:46:54.600
I'm going to take refuge in here.

00:46:57.200 --> 00:47:01.320
It's actually drawn blood from my head!

00:47:02.480 --> 00:47:10.440
And Britain's Big Wildlife Revival starts next Sunday on BBC One at 5:35.

00:47:09.800 --> 00:47:14.520
For me, it's time to say goodbye to the Farnes and get back to my main mission.

00:47:20.600 --> 00:47:27.280
I'm off the coast of Northumberland with Ben Burville.

00:47:26.400 --> 00:47:32.480
Ben's a GP by day but away from the surgery he's an underwater cameraman.

00:47:33.800 --> 00:47:40.760
We're in search of one of the North Sea's best-kept secrets, white-beaked dolphins.

00:47:39.800 --> 00:47:49.920
If Ben captures underwater footage of them it will be a first for British television, never before have these dolphins been shown in UK waters.

00:47:50.640 --> 00:47:56.880
Definitely, dolphin.

00:47:56.040 --> 00:47:59.320
Five o'clock.

00:47:58.440 --> 00:48:00.280
Quarter of a mile.

00:47:59.320 --> 00:48:06.720
There it is, you see it there?

00:48:06.400 --> 00:48:09.400
What is it?

00:48:08.600 --> 00:48:11.240
It's a minke whale.

00:48:10.400 --> 00:48:12.920
It's not a white-beaked dolphin.

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:14.120
No, that's a minke whale.

00:48:13.200 --> 00:48:18.280
It's a great sighting but thrilling as it is to spot a minke whale it's not why we are out here today.

00:48:26.960 --> 00:48:31.800
We head further out into the North Sea to continue our search.

00:48:38.640 --> 00:48:40.000
Why have we stopped, Ben?

00:48:42.440 --> 00:48:48.840
Nobody's seen a dolphin, have they?

00:48:47.960 --> 00:48:54.440
No, we've stopped cos there's quite a few puffins over there in the water.

00:48:53.640 --> 00:48:54.840
Is it a sign there are maybe dolphins around?

00:48:53.880 --> 00:48:57.440
It's a sign there may be food in the water, big sand eels...

00:48:56.600 --> 00:49:01.200
There could be dolphins here as well.

00:49:02.560 --> 00:49:07.880
To use an old landlubber saying - "It's like looking for a needle in a haystack."

00:49:08.160 --> 00:49:14.960
Now, we are rendezvousing with Newcastle University's marine research ship, the Princess Royal.

00:49:14.120 --> 00:49:23.160
Today, Ben is working alongside Simon Laing, whose team is hoping to find out what effect the construction of wind farms at sea has on dolphins.

00:49:22.720 --> 00:49:27.080
And Simon is using sound, not pictures.

00:49:26.680 --> 00:49:28.720
What have we got here, Simon?

00:49:27.880 --> 00:49:30.760
This is a towed hydrophone, it's a special type of microphone that listens...

00:49:30.160 --> 00:49:32.080
A microphone!

00:49:31.200 --> 00:49:37.000
I've seen lots of microphones in my time but never one that looked like this.

00:49:36.240 --> 00:49:41.120
It is a special microphone that listens for sounds underwater.

00:49:40.160 --> 00:49:43.440
But the really clever part is in here.

00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:56.040
Now the microphone's in the water, we can come over to the computer and as soon as we press record, what we will start to see are some of the sounds we are hearing right now, popping up on the screen.

00:49:55.280 --> 00:49:56.520
That's background noise you're seeing on screen.

00:49:55.560 --> 00:49:59.960
What sort of symbol would you see if it was a white-beaked dolphin?

00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:07.520
We would hopefully see a red triangle popping up on screen and that would mean we're recording something in real-time and it would be about 200 metres behind the vessel.

00:50:07.760 --> 00:50:13.360
Can you actually hear the sound of the dolphins?

00:50:12.440 --> 00:50:13.600
Dolphins make two types of sounds.

00:50:12.720 --> 00:50:16.920
They make whistles and clicks.

00:50:16.280 --> 00:50:21.960
We can hear the whistles but we can't hear the clicks.

00:50:22.000 --> 00:50:27.320
That really is a whistle, isn't it?

00:50:26.560 --> 00:50:28.600
Yeah, yeah.

00:50:27.680 --> 00:50:35.520
One of the things we're trying to determine with this project - do the dolphins in the north-east of England have a different whistle to those in Scotland?

00:50:34.720 --> 00:50:42.200
Because that would indicate those two populations are very separate if that is the case.

00:50:41.480 --> 00:50:42.880
And what's the research telling you?

00:50:42.040 --> 00:50:46.280
The research is telling us they may well have different whistles, yeah.

00:50:45.440 --> 00:50:47.680
So, you could have, in this bit of the North Sea, Geordie dolphins?

00:50:46.880 --> 00:50:49.240
With a Geordie accent?!

00:50:48.600 --> 00:50:53.000
Potentially, yes, you could have Geordie dolphins in the north-east.

00:50:53.080 --> 00:51:00.360
Whatever the accent there's not a whistle right now from the white-beaked dolphins so we're, obviously, in the wrong place.

00:50:59.400 --> 00:51:02.560
I'm going to try my luck again with Ben.

00:51:07.640 --> 00:51:08.680
Done it.

00:51:08.040 --> 00:51:09.520
Whoops!

00:51:08.640 --> 00:51:25.360
Ben reckons our best chance of seeing the dolphins lies 18 miles out in the Farne Deeps, where the sea floor will be around 100 metres below us, that's equivalent to the height of Saint Paul's Cathedral.

00:51:34.000 --> 00:51:38.080
We've arrived at our destination.

00:51:37.240 --> 00:51:42.600
All we can do now is keep our eyes peeled and hope.

00:51:46.920 --> 00:51:49.640
There we are, look!

00:51:51.160 --> 00:51:55.000
Straight there!

00:51:57.160 --> 00:51:59.560
It's 15 metres from us.

00:51:59.080 --> 00:52:01.360
OK, here at the front of the boat.

00:52:00.640 --> 00:52:02.080
I see it.

00:52:01.400 --> 00:52:03.960
Can you see it?

00:52:03.040 --> 00:52:04.440
There you are.

00:52:03.520 --> 00:52:05.840
Wow!

00:52:05.040 --> 00:52:09.760
So, this is a white-beaked dolphin and it's choosing to bow-ride.

00:52:08.840 --> 00:52:14.000
One, two, three, four, five, six, seven... eight, nine.

00:52:14.120 --> 00:52:15.880
Wow!

00:52:18.640 --> 00:52:24.400
A beautiful sight, John.

00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:25.760
Isn't it an amazing sight?

00:52:24.880 --> 00:52:27.440
And I can see the white beaks so clearly now.

00:52:30.120 --> 00:52:39.000
What's incredible to me is that these wild creatures want to come so close to our boat and just play around.

00:52:38.160 --> 00:52:47.000
It's illegal to disturb dolphins so Ben's been granted a special licence to dive close to them.

00:52:47.000 --> 00:52:51.400
An aggregation here.

00:52:50.480 --> 00:52:51.840
Several pods, you think?

00:52:50.880 --> 00:52:53.640
Without a doubt, mixing.

00:53:20.680 --> 00:53:25.560
John, I am surrounded by dolphins.

00:53:24.600 --> 00:53:29.520
What an experience.

00:53:28.560 --> 00:53:36.640
When they are under the water they are using an echo location and they are using clicks and whistles.

00:53:35.720 --> 00:53:43.920
And the whistles really are to communicate with each other and... ..the maximum we can hear is about 20 kHz.

00:53:43.000 --> 00:53:45.840
Here we go, I'm just going to have a look at that one.

00:53:44.880 --> 00:53:46.680
OK.

00:54:04.800 --> 00:54:10.360
Quite amazing to see, isn't it?

00:54:09.840 --> 00:54:15.080
Without doubt, some identification of males and females.

00:54:14.200 --> 00:54:17.760
And what do you notice about their behaviour?

00:54:16.880 --> 00:54:19.080
Their behaviour is that they are inquisitive.

00:54:18.240 --> 00:54:24.760
There are amazing wildlife just off our shores here and...

00:54:24.120 --> 00:54:29.600
The information you're gathering underwater could be vital for the future protection of these creatures.

00:54:28.720 --> 00:54:30.080
It certainly could, for the future protection of these creatures.

00:54:29.200 --> 00:54:30.720
Yes, John, you're right.

00:54:37.000 --> 00:54:46.520
Well, it's been a real privilege spending some time here in one of the deepest parts of the North Sea with these wonderful creatures.

00:54:45.680 --> 00:54:54.880
And now, thanks to the underwater footage that Ben's been collecting, we should know a little bit more about their, up till now, secret lives.

00:54:54.080 --> 00:55:02.160
Next week, Countryfile is in Oxfordshire, where, amongst other things, we will be taking part in a tug-of-war with a difference.

00:55:03.640 --> 00:55:05.960
But, from the North Sea, goodbye.
